course: dictionary
lesson: 9886
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word tail is a good example. «The satanic leaf-tailed gecko has a body and tail that look like a dry curled leaf» means the part at the back end of an animal's body, because a gecko has a body and a tail, just as a dog does.
explain: «In Siberia the foxes became friendly and wagged their tails» also uses the animal sense. You can tell because wagging is something a tail does. Now look at «Fish can swim with their other fins and tail». The fish uses its tail to swim, so tail is still a body part here.
explain: Tail can also be a verb, meaning to follow someone closely and secretly. To check which meaning fits, swap your meaning into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have chosen well. Always test the meaning in the sentence, not just the word on its own.
fact: Tail means the part at the back end of an animal's body that sticks out and can often move.
fact: Animals use their tails to balance, swim, steer, swat flies and hold on.
fact: Tail can also mean the back part of something, such as the tail of a plane or the tail of a comet.
fact: Tails is the side of a coin without the head picture, and each side has a one-in-two chance when you flip a coin.
fact: A wagging tail does not always mean a dog is friendly, so look at the whole animal before you decide.
exercise: «The satanic leaf-tailed gecko has a body and tail that look like a dry curled leaf.» In this sentence, what is a tail? | The back of a plane | The side of a coin with no head on it | *The part at the back end of an animal's body | To follow someone secretly
exercise: Mia says, «A wagging tail always means a dog is friendly.» What is the best answer? | Yes, a wagging tail always means friendly | Tails never move | Only cats wag their tails | *No, a wagging tail does not always mean a dog is friendly
exercise: When you flip a coin, what is the chance of getting tails? | *One in two | Never | One in ten | Always
test: «The hyacinth macaw is about a metre long from beak to tail.» In this sentence, what is the tail? | *The back end of the bird | To follow someone secretly | The front of the bird | The side of a coin
test: Which sentence uses tail as a verb? | *The detective began to tail the suspect through the crowd | The tail of the comet glowed | Heads or tails: which side will show? | The lizard dropped its tail to escape
exam: Which sentence uses tail with a different meaning from the others? | Lizards can drop their tail to escape | Fish swim with their tail | *Heads or tails: which side will the coin show? | Cats use their tail to balance
